-- An evidence miscount in a declaration nothing reaches. `f` is an
-- unannotated recursive function with an `eq` constraint; a miscount would
-- pass the recursive call `f x y (n - 1)` a different number of hidden
-- arguments than `f` takes. `main` never uses `f`.
--
-- Dead-code elimination would hide such a miscount from `Lower`, which meets
-- only code it keeps. So the evidence-count assert
-- (checker-v2.md §13.1) walks every declaration at `check` time and
-- would refuse the program with INTERNAL ERROR at the recursive call: the
-- table is wrong whether
-- or not it is emitted. The checker counts
-- correctly, so it builds and prints `x`.
import Node exposing (Program)


f x y n =
    if n == 0 then
        x == y
    else
        f x y (n - 1)


main : Program
main = Node.printLines [ "x" ]
